            <ArticleTitle>Intra-abdominal gout mimicking pelvic abscess.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>229-33</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Gout is the most common crystal-induced arthritis. Gouty tophi typically deposit in the extremities, especially toes and fingers. We present an unusual case of intrapelvic tophaceous gout in a patient suffering from chronic gouty arthritis. CT and MRI of the abdomen and pelvic cavity disclosed calcified gouty tophi around both hips, and a cystic lesion with peripheral enhancement in the pelvic cavity along the course of the iliopsoas muscle. The intra-abdominal tophus mimicked pelvic abscess.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
